This paper investigates the shape assessments for the $B_s^0ightarrow D_s^{*-}\mu^{+}
u_{\mu}$ decay rate, which is observed in data by the LHCb collaboration but lacks a precise theoretical calculation. A comparative study of potential form assumptions for the decay rate distribution is performed, using data for the overall decay rate and the $dQ^2/d\Gamma$ shape, with results for several form hypotheses. A novel approach is also presented that leverages just one kinematic variable, $d\Gamma/dQ^2$, which serves as a constant according to data constraints. A comprehensive theoretical calculation for this decay is highly desirable to enhance sensitivity to certain beyond-Standard Model interactions, specifically those affecting the $bightarrow c
u ar{\mu}$ transition. A proposed strategy for achieving a precise understanding of this decay in the laboratory is also discussed, with a planned precise measurement of the $B_s^0ightarrow D_s^{*-}\mu^{+}
u_{\mu}$ decay rate to inform about new physics. A straightforward implementation is feasible with existing experimental equipment, such as the LHCb detector with a target institution of 3 ab$^{-1}$. A theoretical calculation for this decay will be crucial for developing a comprehensive understanding of $B$ decays. A similar analysis is conducted for the $B_s^0ightarrow D_s^{-}\mu^{+}\mu^{-}$ decay, which is expected to occur approximately 10 times more frequently than the $B_s^0ightarrow D_s^{*-}\mu^{+}
u_{\mu}$ decay. A theoretical calculation for this decay will be crucial for developing a comprehensive understanding of $B$ decays. A similar analysis is conducted for the $B_s^0ightarrow D_s^{-}\mu^{+}\mu^{-}$ decay, which is expected to occur approximately 10 times more frequently than the $B_s^0ightarrow D_s^{*-}\mu^{+}
u_{\mu}$ decay. A theoretical calculation for this decay will be crucial for developing a comprehensive understanding of $B$ decays. A similar analysis is conducted for the $B_s^0ightarrow D_s^{-}\mu^{+}\mu^{-}$ decay, detected by searching for three charged particles with a total charge of $-1$, consisting of two muons and a hadron consisting of a charmed strange meson, which is identified using several variables combining properties of the decay products.
